  "foundations": [],
  "design_notes": [
    "The land surface is evaluated globally before territory ownership or export boundaries are applied.",
    "The western watershed gathers tributaries before Four Gates and splits only in the Manymouth floodplain.",
    "Eastern mineral badlands grade through steppe to coastal downs; the southeastern ridge becomes warm limestone.",
    "Crownwater is an archipelago beyond the delta. Sea crossings need authored ferry or bridge connections.",
    "Foundations can supply center, radius, elevation and feather in metres; they are applied after natural drainage.",
    "Mirror Lake occupies a sheltered basin below the fortress; its outlet descends west into the Western River.",
    "The upper Western River follows low ground west of the spine, preserving a gradual alpine valley rather than excavating a parallel trench.",
    "The Western River bends around the inhabited Four Gates terrace; civic buildings are not used as dams across its channel.",
    "Crownwater lies farther south so its connected causeway city fits its named island territory without extending into mainland server ownership.",
    "Whitehorn Range design O2: design O turned -34 degrees about the gate court and squeezed 0.80 x 0.85, now bounded by a leaf mask on the relief source rather than the crop rectangle; extra ownership sites carry the gate court and the south-east tail to Whitehorn while Amberwood keeps its lodges and far watch tower; the Moors, Amberwood and east passes are authored as connection sites; the leaf mask feathers over 110 m from vertices pulled 50 m in on the west, south-west and south, with the Moor foothills raised to 55-82 m under them, so the flanks fall as a mountainside instead of a mesa wall; and the Hornwater rises at the frozen cascades, cuts the surveyed gorge and its ravine over the shelf east of the gate court (a \"cuts_relief\" river is drained after the relief sources) and joins the Amberwater at its surveyed head level."
  ],
